    bio: "Harry S. Truman was born on May 8, 1884, in Lamar, Missouri, the son of a mule trader and farmer. He was a voracious reader who memorised long passages of history and never lost his passion for the subject, yet he never attended college — a conspicuous anomaly among twentieth-century American presidents. After serving with distinction as an artillery captain in France during the First World War, he returned home to open a haberdashery, which failed, and eventually found his footing in Democratic Party politics in Missouri.\n\nTruman rose through the ranks as a loyal and industrious senator, known for heading a wartime committee that saved billions of dollars by exposing fraud in defence contracts. Franklin Roosevelt chose him as his fourth-term running mate in 1944, and Truman had been vice president for just eighty-two days when Roosevelt died in April 1945. He confessed to reporters that he felt as though \"the moon, the stars, and all the planets\" had fallen on him, and within weeks he faced a decision unlike any in history: whether to use a weapon whose very existence he had barely known about.\n\nTruman's presidency was defined by enormous decisions made in conditions of radical uncertainty. He authorised the bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, launched the Marshall Plan that rebuilt Western Europe, helped establish NATO, recognised the State of Israel within minutes of its declaration, and desegregated the US armed forces by executive order. He is consistently ranked by historians among the near-great presidents — a plain-spoken man from Missouri who found himself at the hinge of the twentieth century and did not flinch."
